<p>A simple proof of the Pythagorean theorem. On each of these identical square tabletops are arranged four copies of the same right triangle. On the first table, the uncovered space takes the shape of the square of the triangle&#8217;s hypotenuse; on the second, it equals the sum of the squares of the triangle&#8217;s two smaller sides. The two tabletops are the same size, so these uncovered spaces have the same area.</p>
